<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khandesh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khandesh is a historical region in the valley of the Tapti river in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, now mainly covered by the Jalgaon, Dhule and Nandurbar districts. From the late 14th century to 1601 it was ruled by the Faruqi sultans from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a>. It then became a Mughal province, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> in the 18th century and was taken by the British in 1818.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khandesh.jpg" alt="Khandesh" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Huge Earth · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Purna_river_of_Muktainagar_in_Jalgaon,_Maharashtra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Khandesh lies in the north-western corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satpura-range">Satpura Range</a> to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> region to the east, the Hills of Ajanta to the south and the northernmost ranges of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ghats">Western Ghats</a> to the west. Its main feature is the Tapti River, which, unlike most rivers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, flows westward to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a>. The Tapti receives thirteen principal tributaries in Khandesh, among them the Girna, Bori and Panjhra, but none is navigable, and its deep bed long made irrigation difficult. The richest tracts lie on the alluvial plain north of the river, while the wooded hills of the north and west are home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil people</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Markandeya Purana and Jain texts call the region Abhiradesa, and inscriptions and oral traditions record the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abhira-people">Abhiras</a> there. In 1295 Ala-ud-din Khilji of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> took Khandesh from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> ruler of Asirgarh, and Delhi dynasties held it until Malik Raja founded the independent Farooqui dynasty in the late 14th century. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, whose army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> overran the region and took Asirgarh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burhanpur">Burhanpur</a> was the capital of the Khandesh Subah. Shah Jahan introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todar-mal">Todar Mal</a>'s revenue system around 1640, and the mid-17th century has been called the time of Khandesh's highest prosperity, owing to trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> and cloth.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> raids began in 1670, and a century of unrest followed until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a> ousted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in 1760 and granted parts of the region to the Holkar and Scindia rulers. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baji-rao-ii">Baji Rao II</a> surrendered to the British in June 1818, fighting continued in Khandesh, one of the last of the Peshwa's former lands to come fully under British control. It became a district of the Bombay Presidency and in 1906 was divided into East Khandesh, headquartered at Jalgaon, and West Khandesh, headquartered at Dhule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a> was divided in 1960, East and West Khandesh became the Jalgaon and Dhule districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, and Dhule was later split to form Nandurbar district. The Khandeshi language, named after the region and sometimes counted as a dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a>, is widely spoken there, and the region is known for growing bananas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khandesh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its wars shaped the balance of power among Malwa, <b>Khandesh</b>, the Bahmanis and the Rajput states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gujarat-sultanate">Gujarat Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dangs, a hilly teak forest between Gujarat and <b>Khandesh</b>, were held by a number of Bhil chiefs, Rajas and Naiks, who raided the plains in the 18th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/dang-states">Dang States</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Khandesh</b> was a buffer state between the larger Gujarat Sultanate, Malwa Sultanate and the Deccan sultanates, and its rulers survived by shifting alliances and marriages.</blockquote><a href="/state/khandesh-sultanate">Khandesh Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
